The diagram illustrates the ReAL-TG framework and the LLM-as-a-Judge system. On the left, the framework starts with a query  $q = (u_q, ?, t_q)$  and a historical graph  $\mathcal{H}_t$ . It uses T-CGS to construct a context graph  $\mathcal{G}_c$ . A Language Model is then used to generate a JSON output containing a prediction  $O: \langle \text{think} \dots \rangle \langle \text{answer} \dots \rangle$ . This output is evaluated using a Link Forecasting Reward  $r(O) = \text{F1}(\{a_{\text{ans}}\}, \{v_q\})$  and a GRPO Objective. The process also involves Verbalization and Reasoning Claims. On the right, the LLM Judge evaluates the JSON output based on Faithfulness, Logical Consistency, and Answer-Explanation Alignment.

